<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="aliphatic glucosinolate biosynthesis, side chain elongation cycle" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWYQT-4450" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Glucosinolates &quot;Glucosinolates&quot;| are secondary metabolites found in 16 plant families, including agriculturally important crop plants of the |FRAME: TAX-3700| such cabbage, mustard, oilseed rape, and broccoli, as well as the model plant |FRAME: TAX-3702|, and are responsible for the typical sharp taste and odor of these plants. The glucosinolates are substituted &amp;beta;-thioglucoside &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-hydroxysulfates that are formed from any one of eight amino acids, namely, |FRAME:L-ALPHA-ALANINE|, |FRAME: VAL|, |FRAME: LEU|, |FRAME: ILE|, |FRAME: PHE|, |FRAME: MET|, |FRAME: TYR| and |FRAME: TRP|. Over 115 naturally occurring glucosinolates have been identified. Much of the diversity amongst glucosinolates arises from the addition of different sized alkyl groups to the side chain of the amino acids, principally valine, phenylalanine and methionine |CITS: [18458837]|. They have been grouped into aliphatic, aromatic, and indolic types, depending on whether they are derived from methionine, phenylalanine and tyrosine, or tryptophan. Indolylmethyl glucosinolates are found mainly in leaves. For a review, see |CITS: [12049923]|. Glucosinolates are stored in the vacuole and come into contact with the enzyme |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.147| (myrosinase) upon breakage of the cell. Myrosinase de-glucosylates the glucosinolates, resulting in formation of nitriles, isothiocyanates and thiocyanates. The precise product that is formed depends on the glucosinolate and whether an epithiospecifier protein is present. The breakdown products give the typical sharp taste and odor to many of the members of brassicaceae that are eaten, such as raddish and mustard. Arabidopsis has been shown to accumulate at least 23 different glucosinolates in both seeds and leaves. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The elongation of |FRAME: MET| starts with its transamination to form a 2-oxo acid (as described in |FRAME: PWY-1186|). The 2-oxo acid is then extended by one methyl group by a condensation with acetyl-CoA, followed by an isomerization reaction and an oxidative decarboxylation reaction. The newly formed 2-oxo acid can be transaminated to homomethionine or undergo further cycles of condensation, isomerization and oxidative decarboxylation to form di, tri, tetra, penta, and hexahomomethionines. Mono, di, tri, tetra, penta, and hexahomomethionines all serve as precursors for aliphatic glucosinolates biosynthesis |CITS: [17369439][16669764]|. This side chain elongation cycle resembles the reaction sequence of |FRAME:LEUSYN-PWY|. Correspondingly, some enzymes of leucine biosynthesis also function in methionine chain elongation and vice versa |CITS: [18162591][19597944][19674406]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
